speechtext:
No, they did not. The position in which the returning officers were placed when they were called on to carry out the election in the province of Manitoba was this :	I have here before
me the lists of Manitoba, nearly all of them, and you can go over these lists from one end to the other and there is not a polling division mentioned throughout the lot-they are all registration divisions, and there is no evidence anywhere that the returning officer could show in any shape or form that there existed a polling division anywhere in the province of Manitoba. That being the case, I take the position that the returning officer did perfectly right in creating his own polls as he saw fit. And that was done. Some of these lists were less suitable for polling divisions than others. Let me cite a case from my own division in which there was no formal allegation that there was any disfranchising by the ' Thin Red Line ' notwithstanding Hon. Robert Rogers, who has been quoted here to-night, said that there were 1.395. I will cite one instance in my own experience. I have possibly one of the most favourable divisions for establishing polling places in the province of Manitoba. Here are two townships and a half in the provincial constituency. At one end there is a town largely Grit, at the other end there is a town that is largely Tory. These are all put into one poll, and the Grits are forced to go from their town twelve miles to the other town to vote. I think the returning officer under those conditions would have the right to divide that poll and make it convenient for the voters. I think the poll is numbered 44. Under such conditions I think the returning officer was justified 256i
in doing everything he did do, and he should not have been subjected to prosecution. The hon. member for Marquette said to-night that no one had been prosecuted for making a division where there was an overlapping of polls. Now, I have here a map showing a case where there is an overlapping of one polling division into the counties of Lisgar, Macdonald and Souris. I have here the federal electoral list. The division is called the provincial electoral district of Mountain, registration district No. 5 ; 41 names apply to Souris, 6 to Lisgar and the balance to Macdonald. Yesterday with the clerk of the Grown in Chancery I checked over this list with the lists that were used at the election by the deputy returning officers in those three polls, and I find that every name was on these lists that were used in that election. But what happened? The returning officer was arrested and prosecuted for dividing that list to suit the circumstances of the case. Mr. Leach, the organizer, was also arrested in connection with the same matter. I am satisfied that so far as the polling divisions are concerned the returning officers were justified in what they did. I have here the lists that are supplied to the returning officers for the electoral division of Portage la Prairie. On those lists there are about 13,000 names, and of those 13,000 names, 4,244 do not belong to the division of Portage la Prairie, and these of course can be put to one side. Then we have in round figures 7,000 names that belong wholly within the territory of the division. Then we have of those that lie partly within the division and partly outside the division names to the number of 1,494, of which 556 are within the bounds of the federal division of Portage la Prairie and 938 outside. Now, here is where the trouble comes in. There are 11 polls that overlap, as shown on a map that I will lay on the table of the House. In poll No. 8 in Beautiful Plains there are 49 names that belong to Dauphin and 145 that belong to Portage la Prairie. In the Gladstone district there are two polls overlapping, in one of which 21 belong to Dauphin and 21 to Portage la Prairie; in the other poll, 15 belong to Dauphin and 38 to Portage la Prairie. In Lakeside there are 90 votes in Macdonald, in poll No. 4, and only 1 in Portage la Prairie. To get a man on list No. 4 for that district you would have either to strike off 90 names and leave the one on, or attach it to some other poll. In poll 3 there are 112 names in Portage la Prairie and 123 in Macdonald. In the original division of Portage la Prairie that overlaps, there are 93 votes in Portage la Prairie and 63 in Macdonald. In Cypress there is a poll overlapping which has 46 names in Portage la Prairie and 164 outside. In South Brandon there are two polls that overlap. In one poll there are 63 in Brandon and 3 in Portage la Prairie ; in the other there

are 34 in Brandon and 56 in Portage la Prairie. In Norfolk there are two polls that overlap. In one there are 86 in Brandon and 17 in Portage la Prairie ; in tlie other poll there are 111 in Brandon and 24 in Portage la Prairie. Out of 556 names there are 123 on these lists in polls that overlap of persons who have not fixed residence, and you cannot tell whether they belong to Portage la Prairie or to Dauphin by looking at the lists.
There is no judge or returning officer who can in any way locate and distribute these names unless he goes to the place and takes evidence. I am surprised that in so far as this particular division is concerned we got off with as little trouble as we had in that election and the reason that things worked out as smoothly in regard to the matter as they did was that all those who were on these lists that had not the proper qualification were left in Portage la Prairie whether they belonged there or not. In regard to the situation and how it works out you will find that in the division of Portage la Prairie there are parts of seven local provincial constituencies and not one whole one. If you want to see a gerrymander, or see a division made to suit political purposes, just look at the constituencies which form part of my division. They run in all kinds of shapes. In every one of them there is more or less trouble in adjusting matters. I took the trouble to go into the situation in so far as the polling subdivisions were concerned, and the working out of things by the returning officer in the Portage la Prairie federal division in the last election. I found that in some of the polling subdivisions for provincial purposes there was only an average of 100-5, but, as arranged for' federal purposes there were 114 people to each poll, and it was a much more convenient arrangement. Everybody was conveniently provided for and nobody had more than six or eight miles to travel to cast his ballot. In connection with the working out of the election law in the province of Manitoba, let me say that our political opponents take the position that at no time has there been any particular exception taken to the preparation and revision of the voters' lists. The hon. member for Marquette (Mr. Roche) is pleased to quote as frequently as possible the position which the Hon. Mr. Meikle took with regard to this election law7 that we have in Manitoba. The way that the hon. gentleman makes the statement misrepresents the facts and misleads the House from the fact that the law that Mr. Meikle referred to has no more semblance to the law that is in existence to-day than a black cat has to a white one. The law that Mr. Meikle referred to was the first law passed by the present government when it came into office in 1900 or 1901. That law Mr. Meikle, I think, approved of, and I am prepared to-night to say that the Mr. CRAWFORD.
provisions of that law met my views more completely than any other law that I know of witli reference to the preparation of the voters' lists. It was practically independent of any political or partisan manipulation; it was free from it altogether. But, that did not suit our Tory friends. They only had one registration under it, that registration was practically fair to all parties, but they could not stand for it and at the very next sitting of the legislature they wiped the whole thing off the statute-book. From section 21 to section 99 of the Act was repealed, entirely doing away with the situation which then existed and placing the preparation of the voters' lists completely in the hands of the executive council of the province, making it purely political and purely under the manipulation of the political machine in power, which was a much worse condition than existed under the legislation of the Greenway government which they declared to be so iniquitous. What is the difference between the two laws ? We find that in regard to the preparation of the lists, as provided for by the Greenway legislation, in so far as the appointing of registration clerks is concerned, the two laws were practically the same. The executive had authority to name the registration clerks and provide for the working out of the registration. There was ample time given, fixed by statute, far more than was given by the present order in council, for the registering of the votes by the registration clerk. Then after the registration clerk did his work the statute fixed a time for the court of revision, not a time for the sitting of the court of revision, but a time in which the court of revision could not sit. The Act provided that when the work of the registration clerk was finished the court of revision could not take place within six weeks. Six- weeks had to elapse from tHe compilation of the list by the registration clerk before the court of revision could be held. Then, what followed? As soon as the registration clerk had finished his work he had to get a complete list of the electors printed as prepared by him. Then, before the court of revision could be held these lists had to be posted up in many places throughout the country, in the school-houses and post offices and they also had to be sent to many officials. Section 22 of the Election Act of 1891 provided that a copy of the list had to be posted up in the following places in the electoral division:
(a)	Every county court office;
(b)	Every post office;
(c)	Every public school house;
(d)	Every municipal hall or office;
And shall mail to the following persons each fifteen copies thereof:
(a) The member for the legislative assembly for the electoral division last elected ;


(b) The defeated candidate for the electoral division at the last election;
And one copy to each mayor, reeve, _ councillor and alderman in the electoral division.
